# Authenticate users with GOV.UK One Login

You must use GOV.UK One Login to authenticate every user your service will issue a credential to. This makes sure the user logged in to the user wallet receiving the credential is its rightful holder. 

You will get a unique client identifier when registering your service with GOV.UK One Login. You must include this identifier as a `clientId` claim in the pre-authorised code you generate when issuing a credential offer.

There is more guidance on [issuing a credential offer](/issue-credentials/credential-offer).

You service will obtain user information for users who authenticate themselves with GOV.UK One Login. This includes their unique subject identifier (`wallet_subject_id`) for your service. You can use this pairwise identifier to carry out a rightful holder check when issuing them a digital credential. This check makes sure the user logged in to your service and the GOV.UK wallet receiving the credential is the same person.

The GOV.UK Wallet subject identifier `wallet_subject_id` links a user to a service, not a device. Your service receives the same `wallet_subject_id` whenever that user interacts with it, even from a different mobile device. If the same user interacts with another service, that service receives a different `wallet_subject_id`. This helps protect the user's privacy across multiple services.

